// What a 'mod' declaration refuses, and the two shapes of it the grammar admits
// and the compiler does not build.
//
// 'mod name;' names the module a source file belongs to, and it claims the whole
// file: it is the file's first statement, and a file declares one module. A 'mod'
// anywhere else is ErrorModDcl, whether it is a second one or merely late.
//
// A nested 'mod name { ... }' block and 'mod trait' are both admitted so that
// their spelling is settled rather than designed later, and both are reported
// where they are written rather than accepted with nothing under them. The nested
// block needs a namespace of its own, a hook pushed and popped around its parse,
// and paths reaching through it, none of which exists.
//
// Parse stage, which is why this is its own file: a parse diagnostic stops the
// compile before name resolution, so nothing here resolves and nothing else could
// be asserted alongside. Each declaration is at the top level, where recovery from
// one reaches the next -- a refused form's body is skipped whole rather than read
// on as global statements.
//
// The accepted header is first, so that everything after it is refused for its own
// reason and not for following a bad one.

mod modparse;

mod inner {                  //~ ErrorUnbuiltKind "nested 'mod' block"
  fn helper() i64 {
    1i64;
  }
}

mod trait Served;            //~ ErrorUnbuiltKind "names a module's abstraction"

mod again;                   //~ ErrorModDcl "first statement"

mod;                         //~ ErrorNoName

// A declaration after the last refusal, so that recovery is shown to land back on
// the global statement stream rather than swallowing the rest of the file.
fn tail() i64 {
  2i64;
}
